How a sandbox session starts, step by step, server side.

Upstream reports four coarse stages (session.sandbox_status), and almost all
of a start happened under the one the web UI calls "Connecting host". This
keeps a finer record of each session's current or last start in
omnigent_startup (images/server/omnigent_startup.py): its steps (create the
sandbox, schedule the Pod, get the runner image, clone each repository, start
the host, run each setup script, start the agent), their times, why a failed
start failed in plain words, and its log. The snapshot carries it as
`startup`, and every change goes out as a session.startup event.
GET /v1/sessions/{id}/startup-log serves the log, and the runner Pod reports
its setup scripts, Tailscale and Docker to POST /v1/hosts/{host_id}/startup
with its launch token (omnigent_startup_api).

Where the steps come from:

- Every stage _publish_sandbox_status publishes goes to the record. The launch
  and wake tasks bind their session (a context variable that asyncio.to_thread
  copies), so the launcher's Pod poll finds it.
- KubernetesSandboxLauncher._wait_for_pod_running already read the Pod every
  2 seconds; it now also reports the PodScheduled condition, image waiting
  reasons, the events of the Sandbox and its Pod, and the workspace-prep log.
  The clone script prints "omnigent-prep: cloning|cloned|failed|kept <name>"
  for each repository, which moves its row.
- _wait_for_host_online reads the host container's log into the startup log
  while it waits.
- The host container gets OMNIGENT_STARTUP_REPORT_URL for the Pod's reports.
- Pod logs are read through _read_pod_log, which decodes the raw body:
  kubernetes 36 hands a plain-text log back as the repr of its bytes
  ('b"line\\n..."'), one line in which no marker matched. Upstream's
  failure log tails read the same way now.

Errors that used to look generic:

- A clone that fails twice ends the start, with the workspace-prep log. A
  Sandbox's Pod never reaches phase Failed, so a bad Git token used to wait
  out the whole Pod budget and fail without git's output.
- "did not create a child pod" now carries the Sandbox's events (quota or an
  admission policy).
- A host that never connects is reported with its container's log, not a
  pointer to /tmp/omnigent-host.log, which only exists for other providers.
- A runner that exits while starting ends the wait at once, with its log tail
  (host.runner_exited), instead of after the 30-second connect timeout.
- A message sent while the sandbox starts waits as long as the start may take:
  the host's online timeout plus the launcher's Pod budget, not 240 seconds.

Cancel start marks the record cancelled before it publishes "ready".
Base: omnigent v0.15.0 (c8b9b85f822f), after 0015-environment-menu.patch.

